  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong heating during the afternoon hours on Thursday \nleads to decent surface based instability for our region. Upper \nlevel shortwave activity will help to sustain some showers and \nthunderstorm activity during the afternoon especially. Maximum \ntemperatures will be in the 80s with dewpoint temperatures in the \n70s, leading to CAPE in excess of 1000 J/kg. Not a lot of deep layer \nshear present, but higher mid level winds associated with trough to \nthe west will help some storms become better organized. Strong winds \nwill be the main threat with any thunderstorms that become better \norganized. Timing for storms entering our northern New York zones \nwill be around 1 pm, with storms exiting Vermont by 8 pm.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heading into the end of this week, warm and unsettled \nweather is expected as the region remains sandwiched between high \npressure to the south and an upper level trough to the north, with \nzonal flow bringing several different disturbances to the region. \nWith broad southwesterly flow ushering in plenty of moisture, \nchances for showers and thunderstorms will be possible. There will \nnot be a defined boundary to help drive shower activity, though the \nshortwave activity Friday and Saturday may provide enough energy to \ndrive afternoon shower and thunderstorm chances. If ingredients \nalign, there could be the potential for some stronger thunderstorms \nboth afternoons, but generally more garden-variety thunderstorms can \nbe expected. Shower activity is expected to wane towards the \nevenings with loss of diurnal heating. High temperatures will rise \ninto the mid to upper 80s with dewpoints around 70 degrees, \nresulting in heat indices in the upper 80s to around 90.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Sunday into Monday it appears the Bermuda high will \nweaken and become suppressed a little farther south and east. As \nsuch, more westerly flow will prevail with less humidity than what \nwill be in place through Saturday. While temperatures will remain on \nthe warm side of normal, the air mass will no longer be muggy and \nprecipitation chances are relatively low through Monday morning. \nSometime in the later Monday or Tuesday timeframe, a cold front will \nzip eastward and bring widespread showers and perhaps some \nthunderstorms, especially if the frontal passage holds off until the \ndaytime on Tuesday. The later timing and much greater instability \ndepicted in one model cluster/scenario, which is consistent with the \n12Z ECMWF, does pose a severe weather threat. At this time, the \nconsensus is Tuesday morning, which limits the risk of impactful \nweather. Per ensemble guidance, below normal precipitable water will \nreturn on Wednesday following a brief surge of deeper moisture on \nTuesday.",
